WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.720
ZAK STONE: Hello, everyone, and
welcome to effective machine

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.400
learning with Cloud TPUs.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.660
I'm delighted to
see all of you here.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.140
And I'd like to send a
special welcome to everyone

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.000
on the livestream or
anyone who's watching

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.770
this as a recording later.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.030
I'm Zak Stone.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.060
I'm the product manager for
TensorFlow and Cloud TPUs

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.660
on the Google Brain Team.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.520
And this talk is
about supercomputers

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.140
for machine learning that are
built around Google's custom

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.070
chips called Tensor
Processing Units, or TPUs.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.960
We've had three generations
of Tensor Processing Units,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.650
including the one on
the bottom that Sundar

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.686
announced in his keynote
earlier this morning.

00:00:42.686 --> 00:00:44.310
But before I get into
the details here,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.320
I just want to set
the stage for a moment

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.599
and share a little bit of
context about why this matters.

00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:52.140
There's been a tremendous amount
of machine learning progress

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.056
over the past several
years, as you've already

00:00:54.056 --> 00:00:56.550
heard earlier today.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.190
If you look at the number
of research publications

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.440
on Archive, which is an open
site for sharing papers,

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.440
the number of papers
published is growing faster

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.505
than Moore's law.

00:01:05.505 --> 00:01:07.380
And we're up to something
like 50 new machine

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.840
learning papers every day.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.460
This is a tremendous
rate of innovation.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.310
A lot of it's happening in
the open, which is fantastic.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:18.120
And it's driving a lot
of new applications

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.330
and real progress across
a wide range of fields.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.330
As you can see here in just
one domain, computer vision,

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:26.490
there's been a
tremendous increase

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.790
in accuracy on this
ImageNet benchmark challenge

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.490
just over the past
few years, rising

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.310
from the original breakthrough
results with AlexNet,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.310
all the way to some of the more
recent machine learning models.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.160
But these accuracy
trends come at a cost

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.230
that I'll tell you
about in a minute.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.840
First, I want to
help you understand

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.660
what those sort of
applications are about.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.420
You've heard a little bit
about diabetic retinopathy,

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.720
and also these new
signals of heart health

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.120
that you can get in a
completely passive way

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.400
just by taking a picture
of the back of the eye.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.190
This is the kind of
real-world application

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.450
that's going to help
improve people's lives

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.290
that's only possible because of
these cutting-edge, accurate,

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.710
powerful models in
machine learning.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.470
You've also probably heard
about tracking illegal logging

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.890
in the rain forest by putting
recycled cell phones in trees

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.220
that can listen for sounds of
chainsaws or other activities

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.980
and help people protect rain
forests in the central Amazon.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.980
Now one thing that's
really changed,

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.810
even since I was
in graduate school,

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.910
is this unification across many
different application domains.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.670
Before, it might have been that
the folks in computer vision

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.340
had one set of techniques, the
folks in speech recognition

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.020
had a different
set of techniques,

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.030
and the folks doing
machine translation

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.300
were doing yet a third
set of techniques

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.137
to go from one
language to another.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:53.220
But what we've been seeing
over the past few years

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.030
is this consolidation,
especially in deep learning

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.700
around these neural
networks that

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.780
have similar components, even if
the details of their structure

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.370
are different.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.870
And these neural networks are
now achieving state-of-the-art

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.300
results across all
these different tasks.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.280
Often these results
are at accuracies

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.010
that we don't know how
to achieve any other way.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:17.310
So now as I mentioned
earlier, this

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.930
has come at a bit
of a cost, which

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.570
is as these models
get more accurate,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.070
they tend to be larger--

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.810
trained on larger datasets--

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.330
and that means that you
need more computation,

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.400
both to train the
model on the dataset

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.390
and then eventually to run it.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:34.740
What I'm showing here is this
plot of these image recognition

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.790
models on ImageNet, this
time ranked with accuracy

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.450
on the vertical axis and then
the number of multiply adds--

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.840
which is sort of a rough
metric of computational costs

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.660
on the horizontal axis.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.230
And as you can see, as you
get to these higher and higher

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.920
accuracies, you're actually
requiring larger and larger

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.760
amounts of computation.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.260
So the solution to this
problem, we believe,

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.120
is specialized hardware
for machine learning.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.910
And that's what's
really driven us

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.190
to develop these multiple
generations of Tensor

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:08.400
Processing Units, or TPUs.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.300
Our first TPUs have been in
our data center since 2015.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.980
And you've interacted with these
every time you've run a search.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.730
They're reranking the
last several hundred links

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.505
to choose the 10 that
they're showing you.

00:04:20.505 --> 00:04:22.380
They've been a central
part of Google Photos,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.420
and speech recognition,
and many of Google's

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.170
large-scale applications
that you use every day.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.050
Last year, we revealed
our second generation

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.930
Tensor Processing Units,
which are now in public beta.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.190
Anyone can sign up.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.640
Any GCP project can
start a cloud TPU.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.640
And you can follow the link
g.co/cloudtpu to learn more.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.100
These TPUs aren't just
for training an inference

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.540
on a single system, though.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.670
They're really designed
to be connected together

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.710
into these large-scale
supercomputers

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.960
that we call TPU pods.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.670
And later this year,
TPU pods are coming

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.250
to cloud as Cloud TPU pods.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.480
Here's a full pod of the second
generation TPUs on the screen

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.670
right now.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:09.330
Finally, this morning, you're
getting the first glimpse

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.700
of our third generation
TPUs, assembled

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.580
into an even larger
TPU pod that delivers

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.800
more than 100
petaflops of compute

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.040
for a single machine
learning problem.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.820
So I just want to
emphasize that we

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.610
are committed to making
relentless progress

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.840
in this domain.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.890
This affects all
of our products.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:31.920
It improves the lives
of all of our users.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.320
And through the cloud, we're
bringing these platforms

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.320
to you, so you can train
machine learning models

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.710
and run them faster and more
cost-effectively than ever

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.330
before.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:43.800
Just a few notes on performance.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:49.160
You can see TPU v1 with 92
teraops only doing inference.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.264
Then going to the Cloud
TPU, which is 180 teraflops.

00:05:52.264 --> 00:05:53.680
Suddenly, you have
floating point.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:54.971
It's a lot easier to work with.

00:05:54.971 --> 00:05:57.090
You don't have to worry
about quantization.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.690
And those are connected
together into these systems

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:04.320
that, as I mentioned before,
are up to 11.5 petaflops,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.990
and do training and inference,
either on a single problem

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.360
or you can slice them
into smaller pieces

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.450
to do many different
problems at once.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.070
Just a year later
now, we're making

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.880
this leap from about 11.5
petaflops to more than 100.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.490
That's more than 8x the
performance of a TPU v2

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.420
pod in this new v3 system.

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:26.340
And that's a combination
of an entirely new chip

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.160
wired together into an
even larger-scale system.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:35.110
So I hope the reason
that you're all here

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:37.600
is because you're interested
in expanding the AI frontier.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.416
That's what really
motivates us when

00:06:39.416 --> 00:06:41.290
we're developing these
platforms and building

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:42.456
applications on top of them.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.880
Now, I'd really like
to focus on what

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.600
you can do right now with the
Cloud TPUs that are available

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.960
today.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.710
And that really starts
with performance.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.330
A lot of these machine
learning models

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.730
can take days or weeks to train.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.620
It hasn't been uncommon
in the past for it

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.240
to cost thousands
of dollars just

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.700
to do one training run of
these machine learning models.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.430
And we're really interested
in bringing down those costs

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:11.640
and making machine learning
acceleration much more widely

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:12.870
available.

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.220
But when we're talking
about performance,

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:19.470
it's important to be very
specific about what we mean,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.570
because it's quite subtle
sometimes to compare

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.700
one system to another.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.040
First of all, in all
of our measurements,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.180
we try to focus on real-world
data, time to accuracy,

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.630
and cost.

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.385
So let me tell you what I mean.

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:35.260
When you're training a
machine learning model

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.680
and you're processing
millions of images,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.560
it's convenient to look at how
many samples a second you're

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.261
processing as a
measure of performance.

00:07:43.261 --> 00:07:44.760
But those samples
per second numbers

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.176
don't matter unless
you ultimately

00:07:46.176 --> 00:07:50.340
get where you need to go, reach
the correct final accuracy.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.620
And so what we're
really looking to do

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:56.160
is, yes, measure how fast the
wheels are spinning on the car,

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.280
but also make sure that the car
gets to the right destination

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.110
and crosses the finish line.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.930
So it's very important
whenever you're

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.630
considering building, buying,
renting a machine learning

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:10.980
system, to make sure that you're
asking about not only steps

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.990
per second or
samples per second,

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:16.920
but also the training
accuracy and the total time

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.410
to accomplish the task that
you really have in mind.

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:24.930
We've also put in
a lot of energy

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:27.030
to try and make sure
that all these ML

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:29.190
benchmarks that
we talk about are

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:31.177
reproducible via open source.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:33.510
So almost all of the numbers
that I'm about to show you,

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:37.080
either you can reproduce today
by going to g.co/cloudtpu

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.870
and renting a Cloud TPU or
they'll be open source soon,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.330
and so you'll be able to
reproduce them on your own.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:45.430
You don't just have to
take my word for it.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:49.840
Now, Stanford recently hosted
a competition called DAWNBench.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:52.030
I've got a screenshot
of the website here.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:55.510
And DAWNBench is the first
public benchmark challenge

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:58.990
that we've seen really measure
performance and accuracy

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:01.310
together, which we
think was great.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:04.210
There was active participation
from many different companies,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.280
and individuals,
and research groups.

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:11.080
And I'm happy to announce
that Cloud TPUs and TPU

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.510
pods did very well across
several of the categories.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.440
So in the ImageNet
Training Cost competition,

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.570
we had the number
one DAWNBench entry

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.600
with a model called
AmoebaNet-D. Now

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:25.120
this is a really interesting
fusion of some of the work

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:26.590
that we've been
doing in research

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:28.006
with these new
hardware platforms,

00:09:28.006 --> 00:09:30.550
because AmoebaNet was an
architecture that was actually

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:33.820
evolved from scratch on TPUs.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:36.550
And then that model, not only
if you train it longer can get

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:40.850
to state-of-the-art
accuracy on ImageNet,

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:45.220
but it can hit the target that
DAWNBench set of 93% in just

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:47.950
7.5 hours for under $50.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:51.359
This really makes this kind of
state-of-the-art performance

00:09:51.359 --> 00:09:53.650
accessible to a much wider
audience than it's ever been

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:55.910
before.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:58.750
Now, another model that you may
have heard of that's frequently

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:01.090
benchmarked is ResNet-50.

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:04.120
And so I just wanted to show a
quick case study of ResNet-50

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.740
running with
TensorFlow 1.8, which

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:07.690
is our most recent version.

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:09.610
We also submitted
this to DAWNBench,

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.810
and it also reaches this
accuracy of 93%, which is not--

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.870
this is top 5 accuracy-- that's
not easy to do with ResNet.

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:18.920
That it's easy to fall
just a little bit short.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.610
And you have to be very careful
throughout the implementation

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.950
to achieve that high
accuracy while maintaining

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.870
this incredible
performance of more

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:28.940
than 3,250 images per second.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.860
Now this took almost nine
hours to train for $59.

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:35.440
But if you want
to go faster, part

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.500
of the reason that we design
these systems to be connected

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:42.940
together is just by changing
the batch size of the model--

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:44.980
no code changes, no
complicated thinking

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:46.900
about distributed systems--

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:50.830
we can run the same thing on
half of one of these TPU pods

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:55.010
to hit the same level of
accuracy in just 30 minutes.

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:56.867
So that's nine hours
down to 30 minutes.

00:10:56.867 --> 00:10:58.450
And these are the
pods that are coming

00:10:58.450 --> 00:10:59.660
to cloud later this year.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:02.470
So you'll be able to run
results like this on your own

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:06.945
or train on other models with
other datasets of your own.

00:11:06.945 --> 00:11:09.070
And if you eliminate the
checkpoint overhead, which

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.140
DAWNBench didn't actually
require us to measure,

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:14.570
that's actually just 24 minutes.

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.950
So what I'd like
to mention, though,

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:21.230
is the field is
moving extremely fast.

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:23.920
So just as an example,
the third place entry

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.480
for training costs
on DAWNBench was

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.840
from a nonprofit
organization called fast.ai.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.690
And that actually
came in at $72,

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.730
but it included some
really interesting ideas

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:35.260
that weren't well-known.

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:38.200
One was this progressive
scaling up of training images

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:41.050
that enabled the model to move
even faster in the earlier

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:43.600
phases of training, when it's
just starting to figure out

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:44.849
how to complete the task.

00:11:44.849 --> 00:11:47.140
And they also used a much
more aggressive learning rate

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:51.214
schedule that hadn't
been widely used.

00:11:51.214 --> 00:11:53.380
The nice thing about Cloud
TPU is it was easy for us

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.460
to make these open changes
in our own model on Cloud TPU

00:11:57.460 --> 00:11:59.950
and unofficially
rerun the experiment.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:02.890
And these two simple changes
take our ResNet-50 training

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:08.110
cost from $59 down to just $25
with today's on-demand pricing

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.167
that you can get
on Google Cloud.

00:12:10.167 --> 00:12:12.250
So there's an open source
reference implementation

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:13.070
available.

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:16.420
We've built this into our
bfloat16 version of ResNet-50.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.370
And we'll have more to
say about this soon.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:22.990
But kudos to fast.ai for the
great algorithmic improvements.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:26.680
And I'm just delighted as a
researcher and practitioner,

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:29.440
and now product manager,
that so much of this progress

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.030
is widely shared.

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:33.830
So you might be
wondering, well, what's

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:35.430
the next benchmark challenge?

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.190
DAWNBench finished recently.

00:12:37.190 --> 00:12:40.550
Where do we go next to compare
these very different systems

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.800
of different sizes and scales
and even different model

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:45.080
architectures
against one another

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.710
if we need to make a decision
about what to do when

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:51.710
you want to accomplish a goal?

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:55.190
Well, one new challenge
that's been released recently

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:56.480
is MLPerf.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.850
You can learn more
about it at mlperf.org.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.850
There are a bunch of researchers
from different top universities

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:04.220
and also many companies
coming together

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.220
to try and define a new next
generation benchmark that's

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:09.140
even more challenging
than DAWNBench

00:13:09.140 --> 00:13:11.850
and covers many more
problem domains.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.760
The first deadline is
going to be in July.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:16.520
This group is open
to new collaborators,

00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:19.604
and also planning to iterate on
the definition of the benchmark

00:13:19.604 --> 00:13:21.020
to make sure that
it really tracks

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.274
where the field is going.

00:13:22.274 --> 00:13:24.440
So I think this is going
to be really exciting later

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:24.940
this year.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:29.330
And I encourage you to
participate and check it out.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:30.830
And as I've mentioned
before, I just

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.163
want to provide a little bit
more information about what

00:13:33.163 --> 00:13:35.930
you can do today with Cloud
TPUs if you want to experience

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.347
this kind of performance.

00:13:37.347 --> 00:13:38.930
We've got the set
of reference models.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:40.471
I'll say more about
them in a minute,

00:13:40.471 --> 00:13:43.790
but they're right there on
github.com/tensorflow/tpu.

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:48.050
And we've also got great
documentation on the Cloud TPU

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:49.497
website with the link below.

00:13:49.497 --> 00:13:51.830
But you don't have to remember
the big links at the top,

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:56.880
just g.co/cloudtpu will take
you where you need to go.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:59.060
So now I'd like to give
you a sense of what

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.056
it's like hands-on to
use these Cloud TPUs.

00:14:02.056 --> 00:14:03.680
First of all, one
thing that's a little

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.270
different than
other accelerators

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:07.010
is that they're
network-attached.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.470
So what that means is you
can choose a virtual machine

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:11.210
of any size or shape.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:14.600
You can attach other
accelerators to it if you want.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:15.697
It can be large.

00:14:15.697 --> 00:14:17.780
But you would generally
start with a very tiny VM,

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:20.390
because this is really playing
just a coordinating role.

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:22.520
As few as four cores
are often enough,

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.170
because all the real
computational work

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:26.750
is happening on the Cloud TPU.

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:28.700
The nice thing
about this setup is

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:31.310
that Cloud TPU can
be a single device

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:33.086
or it can be a slice
of one of those pods.

00:14:33.086 --> 00:14:34.460
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:35.931
All that's handled for you.

00:14:35.931 --> 00:14:37.430
Furthermore, you
don't have to worry

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:38.571
about messing with drivers.

00:14:38.571 --> 00:14:40.820
You can just use these machine
images that we provide.

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:46.066
You program these Cloud
TPUs with TensorFlow.

00:14:46.066 --> 00:14:48.440
Now, TensorFlow is the most
popular open source framework

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:52.130
for machine learning, one of
the top projects on GitHub,

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.830
and it's just taken off since
it was open sourced in November

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:56.030
of 2015.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.730
We're now up to something
like 13 million downloads all

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:00.500
around the world.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.301
TensorFlow supports
many different kinds

00:15:02.301 --> 00:15:04.550
of machine learning and has
many different components.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:06.860
When you're programming
Cloud TPUs today,

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:11.750
you'll focus on layers,
estimators, the tf.data fast

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:14.390
input pipelines,
and then your code

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:17.360
will be transformed behind
the scenes by the XLA compiler

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:20.120
to talk to the TPU.

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:23.240
So estimators and
layers really provide

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:25.640
these flexible high-level
deep learning abstractions

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.910
for TensorFlow that let
you map a wide variety

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:32.644
of different models to TPUs.

00:15:32.644 --> 00:15:34.060
Here's just a quick
code sample of

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:37.000
a convolutional neural network.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.760
And this is how you would write
it with layers and estimators

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.477
with some code elided.

00:15:41.477 --> 00:15:43.060
What you can see
here is that you just

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:46.150
have to make some minimal
changes to map this over

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:47.830
to the TPU--

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:49.925
changes in the optimizer
and the estimator.

00:15:49.925 --> 00:15:52.300
And we're working over time
to make these changes smaller

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.790
and smaller, so that it'll
be really convenient for you

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:58.300
to take TensorFlow code
that runs on CPUs or GPUs,

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:01.690
run it on TPUs, move back and
forth wherever you need to take

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:05.530
your code and train or run.

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:07.540
One important bit
of advice, though,

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:11.230
that I'd like to just
emphasize over and over--

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:14.350
high-performance computing
is hard on any platform,

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:17.080
and these accelerators are so
fast that you need to really

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:20.380
put in a lot of energy to make
sure that the whole system is

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:22.360
balanced and that
every part of it

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:24.820
is performing as well
as it possibly can.

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:28.019
We've done a tremendous
amount of this work for you

00:16:28.019 --> 00:16:30.310
with these reference models
that I'm about to show you,

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:33.690
that we've got open
source on GitHub.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.110
For example, here are
four different categories

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:40.770
of very different applications
that are either supported today

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:44.940
or are about to be open
sourced for Cloud TPU.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:47.070
Over in the image
recognition column,

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:50.490
which is maybe best known, we've
got AmoebaNet and ResNet-50--

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.080
also bigger versions of it--

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:54.360
Inception v2, v3, v4.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:57.699
But we can also do object
detection with RetinaNet,

00:16:57.699 --> 00:17:00.240
which is another one of these
recent state-of-the-art models.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:02.526
And I'll say more
about that in a minute.

00:17:02.526 --> 00:17:04.859
Also if you're interested in
training a machine learning

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:07.200
model and then deploying
it at the edge on a phone,

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:10.680
or a robot, or a drone, or in
a vehicle, or somewhere else,

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:13.109
you might want to train a
smaller model with something

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:14.339
like MobileNet or SqueezeNet.

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:17.339
And Cloud TPUs
support those as well.

00:17:17.339 --> 00:17:20.250
But we're not limited to just
image recognition or object

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:21.089
detection.

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:23.490
TPUs can also do machine
translation and language

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:26.887
modeling, sentiment
analysis, question answering,

00:17:26.887 --> 00:17:29.220
all with this state-of-the-art
model architecture called

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.550
Transformer that's very
powerful and flexible.

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:34.290
Turns out a version
of Transformer

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:37.560
can also perform fantastic
speech recognition.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:39.600
And we've just recently
open sourced an example

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.900
of speech recognition
training on Cloud TPUs

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.820
as part of the
Tensor2Tensor framework.

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:46.840
I'll tell you more
about that in a minute.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:48.870
And finally, for those of you
who are researchers out there

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:51.286
and are at the cutting edge
where you're generating images

00:17:51.286 --> 00:17:55.020
from scratch, over in the
image generation category,

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.290
we've got the Image
Transformer and also DCGAN.

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:02.220
So we've really got a huge range
of applications that are not

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.762
only possible to make
work on the Cloud TPU,

00:18:04.762 --> 00:18:06.720
but they're working today
with open source code

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:08.680
that you can just download.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.750
It's a really easy
way to get started.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.650
So for those of you who aren't
as familiar with this domain,

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:16.650
image classification is often
taking these small images,

00:18:16.650 --> 00:18:20.490
like this one, and mapping
that to a category like "lion."

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.220
Whereas object detection
is taking a larger images

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:24.960
and looking for many
different objects

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:26.849
in many different
places in the image.

00:18:26.849 --> 00:18:28.890
The cool thing about our
RetinaNet implementation

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:31.200
is it lets you process
quite large images--

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:35.430
896 by 896-- trains to a
very interesting accuracy

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:36.660
on the COCO dataset--

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:39.865
37 average precision in
just six hours for $40.

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:46.720
Machine translation training on
the WMT dataset gets to a BLEU

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.780
score of 28.6, which is very
close to the state-of-the-art,

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:53.638
again, in just
6.2 hours for $41.

00:18:53.638 --> 00:18:56.110
And these results are
changing all the time

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:58.900
as we continue to optimize the
platform, the implementations,

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:00.950
and new research comes
out, much of which

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:04.259
at Google is conducted
on these TPUs.

00:19:04.259 --> 00:19:05.800
Now language modeling--
you're trying

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:07.210
to predict the next word.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:09.490
This is an important
component of applications

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.560
like Smart Reply.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:14.830
And again, you can take the
language modeling-- the LM1B--

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:16.750
the one billion word dataset--

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:20.920
and train to a state-of-the-art
perplexity of 28 in 74 hours

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:22.836
for under $500.

00:19:22.836 --> 00:19:24.710
And I've mentioned
Tensor2Tensor again below.

00:19:24.710 --> 00:19:27.340
That's this open source project
built on top of TensorFlow

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.673
that has some of the latest
and greatest results for all

00:19:29.673 --> 00:19:32.605
of these
sequence-to-sequence models.

00:19:32.605 --> 00:19:33.980
For speech
recognition, we've got

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.140
this model called ASR
Transformer, which

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.660
is training on LibriSpeech,
again, to a really

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:40.370
interesting word error rate.

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:42.890
This is a fantastic way if you
have a large labeled speech

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:46.220
dataset to just train your own
near state-of-the-art speech

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:49.370
recognition model from scratch
in whatever language or accent

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:50.600
you care about.

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:55.760
And you can get to a reasonable
accuracy for under $100.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:58.899
Finally, question
answering here with QANet.

00:19:58.899 --> 00:20:00.440
This is just the
fast version that we

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:01.970
used to win the
Question Answering

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:04.910
Competition for DAWNBench, but
there's a Stanford question

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:08.600
and answer dataset that
takes a big chunk of text

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:11.450
and then presents a question
about some fact that you can

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:12.970
infer from reading the text.

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:14.720
So this machine learning
model is actually

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:17.810
looking at this whole
paragraph of unstructured text,

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:20.692
reading the question, and
then pinpointing the answer.

00:20:20.692 --> 00:20:21.650
It's really impressive.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:23.720
It's really different
from image recognition.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:27.320
And all this is possible on
this flexible, new, powerful

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:30.150
platform for machine learning.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.400
Oh, I forgot I was going to
tell you a little bit more

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:33.840
about image generation.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:36.510
So the metric here is
actually bits per dimension,

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:37.530
and lower is better.

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:39.480
State-of-the-art is
around 2.92 right now,

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.130
but under 3 is really good.

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:44.670
And you can get under
3 on this metric

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.717
in 30 hours for under $200.

00:20:46.717 --> 00:20:48.300
If you're interested
in exploring this

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:52.620
state-of-the-art, cutting-edge
image generation examples,

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.430
all those images on the right
were generated from scratch.

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:58.440
Just dreaming on the
basis of a training

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.390
set using this image
transformer model.

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:06.450
So again, why start
with a reference model

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:08.280
instead of just
opening a code editor

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:09.587
and starting from scratch?

00:21:09.587 --> 00:21:11.670
Well, these reference
models are high performance,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:14.010
they're open source,
they're cutting-edge,

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:15.930
often collecting
input from researchers

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.880
all across Alphabet.

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:20.894
We continuously test them
for performance and accuracy,

00:21:20.894 --> 00:21:22.560
which means that you
don't have to worry

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:24.990
that some subtle
change in TensorFlow

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:28.980
or some other part of the
stack might reduce the accuracy

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:30.351
without you realizing it.

00:21:30.351 --> 00:21:32.100
You can get up and
running really quickly,

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.266
and then modify as needed
because you have the code.

00:21:34.266 --> 00:21:35.970
It's yours on your
infrastructure.

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:39.150
And you can train and
run on your own data.

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:42.122
But you're perfectly welcome
to do it the hard way.

00:21:42.122 --> 00:21:43.830
We have lots of detailed
technical advice

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:45.510
in our troubleshooting guide.

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:50.010
And you know, I'm not kidding
that high-performance computing

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:52.860
is hard and any part of the
system can become a bottleneck,

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:57.030
but we totally encourage you
to take the high-level layers

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:59.850
and estimators APIs
and build new models

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:03.250
and let us know
what works for you.

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:05.250
Just to give you a little
bit of a sense of what

00:22:05.250 --> 00:22:07.920
makes high-performance
computing so tricky,

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:10.110
if you're training
with slower hardware,

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:12.480
you might find that you
can overlap your input

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:14.820
preprocessing with
your training time,

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:17.760
and so your accelerators
are always saturated.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.187
But when the accelerators
get a lot faster,

00:22:20.187 --> 00:22:22.020
what you find all of a
sudden is that you're

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:23.520
bottlenecked on the
input processing

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:26.040
and your accelerators
are sitting idle.

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:32.100
And so fortunately, we've
provided a bunch of tools that

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.860
are connected into TensorBoard--
which is this great

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:37.740
visualization framework
associated with TensorFlow--

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.870
to help you debug
your performance,

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:41.010
understand what's going on.

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:43.680
Here, I'm showing the steps
per second and the loss.

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:46.410
But we actually have this
very detailed profiler

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:49.470
that lets you go in and get
op-by-op statistics on what's

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.000
going on in your model.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:53.031
And we even have
high-level recommendations.

00:22:53.031 --> 00:22:54.780
I don't know if you
can read the red text,

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:56.610
but it's telling
you in this case,

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.860
your model is not
input-bound, so you

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:01.800
should spend more time
on optimizing the step

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:02.869
performance.

00:23:02.869 --> 00:23:05.160
In other cases, it'll tell
you you're blocked on input.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.260
Don't bother improving
the speed of your model.

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:09.390
You need a faster
input pipeline.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.190
And that's where
tf.data can help.

00:23:11.190 --> 00:23:14.760
So we're really doing
our best to present

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:18.930
these tools for experts and all
this documentation for experts,

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.000
but also to make it
really easy to get started

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.083
with these reference models
across this huge range

00:23:23.083 --> 00:23:25.180
of applications.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:27.431
Now, those of you who are
used to training and running

00:23:27.431 --> 00:23:29.263
machine learning models
under your desk, you

00:23:29.263 --> 00:23:31.500
might wonder, well, why
should I jump to the cloud?

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.720
That seems like a
different platform.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:35.340
How do I think about that?

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:37.500
The nice thing about
training in the cloud

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:40.387
is that you get access to all
this top-notch infrastructure

00:23:40.387 --> 00:23:42.970
where you can mix and match the
different components you need,

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:46.134
like those custom VM shapes I
was telling you about earlier.

00:23:46.134 --> 00:23:47.550
You can scale up
and down quickly.

00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:49.170
You don't need to
order hardware,

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:53.160
maybe build a machine yourself,
wait for months sometimes

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:54.540
for hardware to arrive.

00:23:54.540 --> 00:23:58.110
You can just sign on today and
scale up to hundreds of TPUs

00:23:58.110 --> 00:23:59.710
if you wish.

00:23:59.710 --> 00:24:02.190
There's great security all
throughout Google Cloud.

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:04.800
Google is state-of-the-art
in security across the entire

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.830
platform for enterprises that
want cutting-edge machine

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:09.090
learning hardware.

00:24:09.090 --> 00:24:12.704
And you're really able to
reduce your capital expenditure.

00:24:12.704 --> 00:24:14.370
With this on-demand
pricing, you're just

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:16.140
paying only for what you use.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:18.159
You can train one of
these models for $50,

00:24:18.159 --> 00:24:20.700
and then turn the cloud TPU off,
and you're not paying for it

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:23.320
anymore, which is great.

00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:27.610
So just a few words from two of
our customers for Cloud TPUs.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:29.760
Two Sigma is a financial firm.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:32.940
And Lyft, this is their
self-driving vehicle team.

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:34.890
Both of them have
had great experiences

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:38.196
using Cloud TPUs to focus
on building the models

00:24:38.196 --> 00:24:40.320
instead of worrying about
these massive distributed

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:43.050
clusters of other
hardware, and taking

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:47.520
these models that used to
train for days in some cases,

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:48.370
down to hours.

00:24:48.370 --> 00:24:50.130
And with these larger
and larger pods,

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.004
we're really trying to
drive that to minutes.

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:57.450
So let's go under the hood
and talk in a little bit

00:24:57.450 --> 00:25:00.300
more detail about what's going
on in the current generation

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:02.520
of Cloud TPU.

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:05.910
So first of all, the Cloud
TPU actually includes a host

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:08.160
with this accelerator,
all behind the scenes,

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:10.300
connected via PCI.

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:13.750
Each Cloud TPU device has these
four chips under the heat sinks

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:14.250
here.

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.980
And I'm going to zoom
in on one of them.

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:19.110
If you look at the
Cloud TPU chip layout,

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:22.560
you'll see that it's broken
down into two cores that

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:23.950
look like this.

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:26.340
Now, these cores
are really built

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:29.410
around this systolic array,
which is an old idea,

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:31.590
but it's newly relevant
in this age of machine

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.720
learning when so many
of the computations

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.270
really look like gigantic
matrix multiplies.

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:38.910
But we've also got a vector
unit and a scalar unit.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:43.530
And in this case,
it was 8 gigabytes

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:45.460
of high-bandwidth
memory per core.

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:47.130
And then there are
two cores per chip.

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:49.360
The new TPU v3 actually
doubles the memory

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:52.110
per chip, which is going to let
you train even larger and more

00:25:52.110 --> 00:25:54.480
capable models.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.907
Focusing in on this matrix
unit, this is a systolic array.

00:25:56.907 --> 00:25:58.740
One thing that's really
interesting about it

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:00.531
is that we're actually
doing the multiplies

00:26:00.531 --> 00:26:03.990
with a new floating point
format that's called bfloat16.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:05.560
I'll say more about
that in a minute,

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:08.220
but it really gives us
this great performance

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:12.300
for machine learning without
a meaningful cost in accuracy.

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:15.210
We're still accumulating
in float32, though.

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.250
So most of you are
probably familiar

00:26:17.250 --> 00:26:18.990
with 32-bit floating point.

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:22.800
As you can see here,
the orange mantissa bits

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:25.380
are all devoted to measuring
these fine differences

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:27.660
between numbers, which is
really important if you're

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:29.460
doing high-performance
computing,

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:31.789
detailed simulations,
that kind of thing.

00:26:31.789 --> 00:26:33.330
But in machine
learning, it turns out

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:34.980
that the range is
much more important.

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:36.354
And these machine
learning models

00:26:36.354 --> 00:26:40.260
are not super sensitive to these
fine differences by and large.

00:26:40.260 --> 00:26:45.420
So the IEEE standard for
a 16-bit floating point

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.527
truncates both the
exponent and the mantissa,

00:26:47.527 --> 00:26:48.360
as you can see here.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:50.070
And it really cuts
down the range

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:51.930
of numbers that you can express.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:54.270
One thing that we're
finding with TensorFlow

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:58.034
is if you're training with
half-precision IEEE like this,

00:26:58.034 --> 00:26:59.700
you really have to
pay attention and you

00:26:59.700 --> 00:27:02.610
need to get an expert involved
on a model-by-model basis

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:05.520
to make sure that all of your
numbers stay in the right range

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.020
and don't overflow.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:10.440
And those techniques are
difficult to apply in general

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:12.618
across a wide range of models.

00:27:12.618 --> 00:27:15.330
With bfloat16, though,
what we've done instead

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:17.760
is saved a lot more bits
for the exponent, which

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:19.230
preserves the range.

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:20.970
And so in many
models, this is a drop

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:22.920
in replacement for float32.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:26.250
And it's supported seamlessly
by our Cloud TPU hardware.

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:28.830
So those ResNet-50 results that
I was talking about earlier,

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:30.450
those were achieved
with bfloat16.

00:27:30.450 --> 00:27:34.080
And as you notice, we're
still achieving that high 76%

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:36.990
top one, or 93%
top five accuracy,

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:39.570
with a minimum of effort
and special casing

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:42.370
on a model-by-model basis.

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:44.550
So here, I'll show
you a brief animation

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:45.660
of the systolic array.

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:50.910
In this slide, it's only 3 by 3,
but imagine this is 128 by 128

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:52.620
to get a sense of
how different this

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:55.280
is from an ordinary
central processing unit,

00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:57.780
and how much parallelism there,
which is where we're getting

00:27:57.780 --> 00:27:59.980
this fantastic performance.

00:27:59.980 --> 00:28:02.550
So here you have
data streaming in.

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:05.160
And what's happening as the
systolic arrays sort of beats

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:08.310
on every step is you're
getting great reuse

00:28:08.310 --> 00:28:10.620
of these intermediate
results as the data

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:12.180
is flowing through
the systolic array

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:15.080
and generating the outputs.

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:17.740
So this is super high throughput
for matrix multiplications

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:20.380
and is tightly integrated with
the other parts of our TPU

00:28:20.380 --> 00:28:22.410
that support all these
other custom operations.

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:25.420
And our XLA compiler
also does a great job

00:28:25.420 --> 00:28:28.490
of taking high-level
mathematical expressions

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:31.505
and then mapping them down
into this coordinated dance

00:28:31.505 --> 00:28:33.880
of low-level operations that
gives this tremendous amount

00:28:33.880 --> 00:28:36.000
of speed.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:39.160
So where do we go from here?

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:42.030
Well, first of
all, I really want

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:46.900
to emphasize something I
haven't called out explicitly

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:48.730
about scaling up.

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:52.340
I think the days of single
systems are irrelevant.

00:28:52.340 --> 00:28:54.640
They're over because
what you care

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:59.740
about, ultimately, is how fast
you get to your result, right?

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:03.010
And so what we're trying to
do with these Cloud TPUs,

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:06.070
and making them widely
available in the cloud,

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:08.020
is giving you just
a dial that you

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.390
can turn where you
do your prototyping

00:29:10.390 --> 00:29:14.140
on an inexpensive single device,
and then you increase the batch

00:29:14.140 --> 00:29:16.735
size without doing any
other code changes,

00:29:16.735 --> 00:29:18.610
and suddenly your training
time is going down

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:21.550
from hours to minutes.

00:29:21.550 --> 00:29:26.600
And so I'll show you two real
measurements from our TPU

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:28.360
systems in-house
here to give you

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:30.860
a sense of how that's possible.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:33.314
First of all, what I'm
demonstrating here,

00:29:33.314 --> 00:29:34.730
if you look at
these TPU devices--

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:39.550
so the second generation TPU pod
has 64 of these boards in it--

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:42.970
and you can see linear
scaling of images per second

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:44.177
up and to the right here.

00:29:44.177 --> 00:29:46.510
And as you know, remember if
you hear images per second,

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:48.490
it's like, but wait,
what about the accuracy?

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:49.989
You can check our
DAWNBench results.

00:29:49.989 --> 00:29:51.670
That 30-minute
result also confirms

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:56.030
that we can still get the high
accuracy with this scale out.

00:29:56.030 --> 00:29:58.570
But what you can see is that
our interconnect in this pod

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:02.200
is so fast that you don't have
to struggle the way you would

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:05.380
with mixing and matching
hardware on your own,

00:30:05.380 --> 00:30:07.480
whether on premise
or in the cloud.

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:09.670
You can just turn the
dial with no code changes,

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:13.420
and benefit from this super
fast proprietary network.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:15.280
But there's a more
subtle point here

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:18.850
that's a mathematical issue,
which is until recently, it

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:21.550
hasn't been clear how to take
these machine learning models

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:24.424
and increase the
batch size, which

00:30:24.424 --> 00:30:25.840
is how many of
these images you're

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:28.990
processing simultaneously
in each step

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:30.340
while you're training.

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.470
There's been a huge
amount of research energy

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:33.640
on this recently.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:35.389
And there have been a
lot of breakthroughs

00:30:35.389 --> 00:30:37.450
where we're now able
to train effectively

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:40.360
on much larger batch
sizes from ever before.

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:42.749
That's great news for scaling
up with these TPU pods.

00:30:42.749 --> 00:30:44.290
Let me help you
interpret this graph.

00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:49.510
What you see on the
horizontal axis here is time.

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:53.230
And then what you see on the
vertical axis is accuracy.

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:56.440
And so you might imagine
your first implementation

00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:59.590
on your Cloud TPU that
you're renting today gets to,

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:02.604
let's say, 76%
accuracy on ResNet-50.

00:31:02.604 --> 00:31:04.270
And that takes a
certain amount of time.

00:31:04.270 --> 00:31:06.730
That's that orange
curve out there.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:10.245
But with no code changes, just
by changing the batch size

00:31:10.245 --> 00:31:12.370
in the right way with the
models that we've already

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:14.530
open sourced, you
can pull that time

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:18.130
in as you're using larger and
larger sections of the pod

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:21.910
until you get down to these
30-minute results, or possibly

00:31:21.910 --> 00:31:23.350
even better.

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:26.515
So we think this is an extremely
promising research direction.

00:31:26.515 --> 00:31:29.140
And we're doing our best in all
these reference implementations

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:31.600
to make sure that it's
immediately accessible to you.

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:34.720
So once these pods become more
widely available in the cloud,

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:37.120
then you'll be able to
just transparently benefit

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:38.656
from this large-batch training.

00:31:38.656 --> 00:31:40.030
I'd love to live
in a world where

00:31:40.030 --> 00:31:41.742
you can use an
exaflop for a minute,

00:31:41.742 --> 00:31:43.450
and then let it go
and think for a while,

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:47.930
while somebody else is using it,
and just swap back and forth.

00:31:47.930 --> 00:31:53.100
Now, here's another view
of the full TPU v3 pod

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:55.460
that Sundar showed
you this morning.

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:57.880
And we are doing
everything that we

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.350
can to push the
boundaries of performance.

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:04.541
And that's what's really led
us to liquid cool this pod,

00:32:04.541 --> 00:32:06.790
in comparison to everything
that we've done previously

00:32:06.790 --> 00:32:08.720
that's air cooled.

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:12.190
If you want a close-up
view, here it is--

00:32:12.190 --> 00:32:15.186
a close-up view of just one
of the boards in that pod.

00:32:15.186 --> 00:32:16.310
It looks sort of like this.

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:18.010
And you can see the tubing.

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:19.810
Now, those plates
are on each one

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:24.190
of these next-generation
TPU v3 processors.

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:27.700
So we're going to go to the
largest batch sizes we can.

00:32:27.700 --> 00:32:30.346
We're publishing all this
research that we're doing.

00:32:30.346 --> 00:32:31.720
We're open sourcing
as much of it

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:35.350
as we can on these
Cloud TPU platforms

00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:37.780
that you can access today.

00:32:37.780 --> 00:32:41.380
And so we're really
excited in going with you

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:44.440
into this exciting AI frontier.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.110
You can get started right now
with these high-performance

00:32:47.110 --> 00:32:48.970
reference models and tutorials.

00:32:48.970 --> 00:32:51.200
And please reach out to us.

